## The one thing to understand

The anon key ships to every browser. Anyone can open devtools, copy it, and
query the REST endpoint directly with curl. This means **Postgres grants and row
level security together are the security boundary of this application.** Checks in server
components, route handlers, and middleware are conveniences on top of it. If RLS
is wrong, none of them matter, because the attacker does not go through your app.

Design every feature by asking what the RLS policy is first, and only then how
the UI calls it.

## Grants, RLS, policies: one unit

Two layers decide what a browser holding the anon key can reach. **Grants**
decide whether a role can reach a table at all. **RLS** decides which rows it
sees once it can. Since Supabase's Data API change (new projects from
2026-05-30, all existing projects from 2026-10-30), new tables in `public` get
no grants automatically, so both layers are written by hand, together.

Every migration that creates a table carries all three, in this order:

```sql
create table public.documents (
  id uuid primary key default gen_random_uuid(),
  owner_id uuid not null references auth.users(id),
  title text not null
);
create index on public.documents (owner_id);

-- 1. grants: only the roles and privileges this table genuinely needs
grant select, insert, update, delete on public.documents to authenticated;
grant select, insert, update, delete on public.documents to service_role;

-- 2. RLS
alter table public.documents enable row level security;

-- 3. policies, one per operation
create policy documents_select on public.documents for select to authenticated
  using ((select auth.uid()) = owner_id);
create policy documents_insert on public.documents for insert to authenticated
  with check ((select auth.uid()) = owner_id);
```

- **Grant narrowly.** `anon` gets nothing unless the logged-out experience
  needs it, and then usually only `select`. `authenticated` gets what signed-in
  users do. `service_role` gets what server code does.
- **Sequences need their own grant** if an insert through the Data API uses a
  `serial` or identity column: `grant usage, select on sequence ... to authenticated;`
- **Views need grants too**, and `with (security_invoker = on)`.
- **Server-only tables get no grants**, and the migration carries a
  `-- @no-data-api` comment so the harness knows it was deliberate.
- **Never fix a missing grant with a bulk grant** (`on all tables in schema
  public`) or by restoring default privileges. Both re-expose every future
  table, which is the problem the platform change exists to solve.
- A `42501 permission denied for table` error from supabase-js means a missing
  **grant**, not a wrong policy. RLS failures return empty results instead.

## RLS rules

- Every table in `public` that has any grant has RLS enabled. Enable it on
  server-only tables too; it costs nothing and protects against a future grant.
- Write policies as narrowly as the feature allows. `using (true)` is the same as
  having no RLS, so if you write it, say out loud why.
- Separate policies per operation and name the role with `to authenticated`.
  A single `for all` policy usually means the read rule and the write rule got
  conflated, and the write rule should be stricter.
- Never let `anon` write without a considered reason. If a contact form needs it,
  scope the grant and the policy to that one table and add a rate limit.
- Wrap function calls in policies so they are evaluated once rather than per row:
  `(select auth.uid()) = user_id` rather than `auth.uid() = user_id`.
- Index every column a policy filters on. Policies run per row, so an unindexed
  `user_id` turns every query into a sequential scan the moment the table is real.

## The service role key

- It bypasses RLS completely. Treat it like the root password, because it is.
- It lives only in files that `import 'server-only'` at the top, which turns a
  bad import into a build error rather than a silent leak.
- Never in a client component, never in a `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable, never in an
  edge middleware, never in a Cloudflare Worker that is publicly reachable.
- Reach for it only when RLS genuinely cannot express the rule, such as an admin
  backfill. When you use it, the query must carry its own ownership filter,
  because nothing else will.

## Auth

- On the server use `getUser()`, never `getSession()`. `getSession` reads the
  cookie and trusts it. `getUser` revalidates against the auth server. A forged
  cookie defeats the first and not the second. The harness blocks `getSession()`
  in server code; the one legitimate exception is reading the access token to
  forward to another service, and for that write `// @allow-getsession` with the
  reason on the line above.
- Derive identity from the verified user, never from an id in the request body.
- `auth.users` stays out of the `public` schema. Keep a `profiles` table keyed by
  `auth.uid()` and expose that instead.

## Functions and views

- `SECURITY DEFINER` functions bypass RLS. Use them rarely, and always with
  `set search_path = ''` and fully qualified object names, otherwise a caller can
  shadow the tables you reference.
- Views run with the definer's rights unless you set `security_invoker = on`.
  A view over an RLS-protected table without it is a hole. Always set it.

## Storage

- Buckets are private by default. Keep them that way and serve through signed
  URLs with short expiry.
- A public bucket means every object in it is readable by URL forever, including
  after you delete the row that referenced it.
- Storage has its own RLS policies on `storage.objects`. Write them.

## Migrations

- Create every migration with `supabase migration new <name>`, so its version is
  a fresh timestamp that sorts after everything already merged.
- **Never edit a migration once it has merged.** Applied migrations are never
  re-run, so production keeps the old version while fresh environments get the
  new one. Put the change in a new migration.
- Never delete a merged migration. Undo its effects with a new one.
- Supabase's GitHub integration applies migrations at merge. Do not apply them
  to any remote database by hand or from a script.
- All schema change goes through `supabase/migrations`. Changes made in the
  dashboard exist in exactly one environment and vanish from history.
- Generate types after every migration and commit them, so drift between the
  database and the TypeScript is visible in the diff.
- Destructive changes go in their own migration, expanded first and contracted in
  a later deploy.

